Profilarr's automatic renames keep your existing library's file and folder names up to date as metadata and naming formats change. You might have added a movie as `Untitled The Batman Sequel (2027)`, only for it to become `The Batman Part II (2028)` while its folder kept the old name. Rather than having to find and rename those items yourself, you can schedule Profilarr to do it for you.

<Callout type="info">

Profilarr doesn't rename anything itself. It asks Radarr or Sonarr to run their own renames, using the naming formats configured there.

</Callout>

## Automatic Renames

Each Arr instance has its own rename settings under **Renames**. Enable **Status** to turn on automatic file renames, and enable **Folders** if you want movie or series folders renamed as well. Save your settings before running either **Dry Run** or **Run Now**.

<ThemeImage
	light="/images/docs_renames[style=light].png"
	dark="/images/docs_renames[style=dark].png"
	alt="The Renames tab for the Movies instance, with renames and folders enabled, a weekly schedule, an Ignore Tag field, and Dry Run, Run Now, and Save buttons."
	border />

**Dry Run** lets you check the proposed filenames first, but it doesn't show folder changes. **Run Now** applies the renames.

If you have items with names you want to keep, set an **Ignore Tag** and add it to those movies or series in the Arr. They won't be renamed.

## Scheduling

Pick a schedule from the dropdown, or choose **Custom** to write a cron expression. [Crontab Guru](https://crontab.guru/) helps you check an expression, and its [syntax reference](https://crontab.guru/crontab.5.html) explains how to write one. Schedules use Profilarr's [timezone](/installation#general).

Renames default to daily at midnight, with at least ten minutes between runs.
